\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Comparison of the recoil from the AK-47 in-game with its theoretical model, implying that the recoil is deterministic and can be compensated for with a script.}}{5}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Comparison of wallhack demonstration using built-in command "r\_aoproxy\_show 1" in CS2, which forces the engine to render near entity models regardless of occlusion (top) and an ad from a cheating software provider showing wallhack in action (bottom).}}{7}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Radarhack demonstration showing the extraction of enemy positions from the game's entity list and their projection onto a secondary interface.}}{8}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Privilege \gls {rings} in an OS' structure, highlighting the elevated access of kernel-level cheats in Ring 0 compared to user-mode applications in Ring 3.}}{8}{figure.caption.7}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Infrastructure of a DMA cheat setup, where a specialized PCIe card reads the host system's RAM directly and transmits the data to an external machine for processing and input spoofing.}}{10}{figure.caption.8}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Sensitive value ("Mot de passe : Test1234!") entered into a Notepad process left resident in memory on the isolated Windows 10 virtual machine.}}{17}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Windows-Kernel-Explorer process list, showing the target notepad.exe process isolated by its PID, PPID, EPROCESS pointer, and executable path.}}{17}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Memory map of the notepad.exe process returned by Windows-Kernel-Explorer, listing several hundred regions by base address, size, allocation state, and protection flags.}}{18}{figure.caption.11}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Cheat Engine string scan of notepad.exe's memory for "Mot de passe", returning a single matching address.}}{19}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Raw hexadecimal bytes at the matched address, viewed through Windows-Kernel-Explorer's memory editor, showing the target string split across three contiguous rows.}}{19}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Reconstruction of the original string via a hexadecimal-to-ASCII converter, confirming an exact match with the value entered in Notepad.}}{20}{figure.caption.14}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces Confusion Matrix for Suspicion Scoring. Montre que l'algo de scoring n'arrive pas à différencier les profils de joueurs casual et expert. Il n'arrive pas également à différencier les profils de joueurs expert et cheater. Il est donc nécessaire de générer des profils plus réalistes pour que l'algo puisse apprendre à différencier les différents types de joueurs.}}{23}{figure.caption.15}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13}{\ignorespaces Flag Frequency Distribution. Montre que l'algo de flagging se déclenche trop souvent pour les cheaters et pas assez pour les expert et les casuals. Je dois donc ajuster les seuils de déclenchement des flags pour avoir une représentation plus équilibrée et réaliste.}}{24}{figure.caption.16}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14}{\ignorespaces Score Distribution. Montre la distribution des scores attribués par l'algo de scoring. Le résultat est relativement satisfaisant pour le moment mais les scores sont globalement trop bas pour les experts et cheaters, donc les stats vont en moyenne être plus suspectes que le résultat actuel.}}{25}{figure.caption.17}%
